 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **Self-hosted WebSockets for change delivery.** Delivery maintains a socket session per browser and publishes each committed revision to subscribers of that event; the change store keeps replayable revisions and publishes reliably, and browsers reconnect and catch up after a disconnect. Five-second HTTPS polling and a hosted realtime service were the alternatives. Polling was rejected for its added lag and repeated reads; a hosted service was rejected to avoid recurring fees and provider coupling. Self-hosting accepts connection-capacity and reconnect operations in exchange for fast updates without a realtime vendor. See [`docs/decisions/change-delivery-approach.md`](decisions/change-delivery-approach.md) for the full tradeoff.
